### Indexed Sequential File (ISAM)

**Theory:**
Indexed files allow fast key-based access while maintaining sequential capability.

```cobol
       ENVIRONMENT DIVISION.
       FILE-CONTROL.
           SELECT EMP-FILE ASSIGN TO 'employees.dat'
               ORGANIZATION IS INDEXED
               ACCESS IS DYNAMIC
               RECORD KEY IS EMP-ID
               ALTERNATE RECORD KEY IS EMP-EMAIL
                   WITH DUPLICATES.
       
       PROCEDURE DIVISION.
       
       READ-BY-KEY.
           OPEN INPUT EMP-FILE.
           
           MOVE "E12345" TO EMP-ID.
           READ EMP-FILE
               INVALID KEY
                   DISPLAY "Employee not found"
               NOT INVALID KEY
                   DISPLAY "Found: " EMP-NAME
           END-READ.
           
           CLOSE EMP-FILE.
       
       SEQUENTIAL-READ.
           OPEN INPUT EMP-FILE.
           
           MOVE "E00000" TO EMP-ID.
           START EMP-FILE 
               KEY IS >= EMP-ID
               INVALID KEY
                   DISPLAY "No matching records"
           END-START.
           
           PERFORM UNTIL WS-EOF = 'Y'
               READ EMP-FILE NEXT RECORD
                   AT END
                       MOVE 'Y' TO WS-EOF
                   NOT AT END
                       DISPLAY EMP-NAME " - " EMP-EMAIL
               END-READ
           END-PERFORM.
           
           CLOSE EMP-FILE.
```

**Key Points:**
- ✅ RECORD KEY is primary index
- ✅ ALTERNATE RECORD KEY for secondary access
- ✅ ACCESS IS DYNAMIC for flexible reading
- ✅ START positions file for sequential read
- ✅ READ NEXT for sequential after START

