## Data Types & Variables

### Theory: COBOL Data Definition (PIC Clause)

**PIC Clause Components:**

```
PIC Clause = Picture Character String

Components:
├─ 9 = Numeric digit (0-9)
├─ X = Alphanumeric character
├─ S = Sign (+ or -)
├─ V = Decimal point position (implied)
├─ P = Decimal point (explicit)
├─ Z = Suppress leading zeros
├─ . = Decimal point (explicit)
└─ * = Fill with asterisks

Modifiers:
├─ (n) = Repeat n times
├─ COMP = Binary (compressed)
├─ COMP-3 = Packed decimal
└─ COMP-5 = Native binary
```

### Practical Examples: Data Type Declarations

```cobol
       DATA DIVISION.
       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
       
       *> NUMERIC DATA TYPES
       01 SIMPLE-NUMBERS.
           05 INTEGER-VALUE        PIC 9(5).
               *> Allows: 0 to 99,999
               *> Example: 12345
           
           05 DECIMAL-IMPLICIT     PIC 9(5)V99.
               *> Allows: 0 to 99,999.99
               *> Example: 12345 = 123.45 (decimal position implied)
               *> Storage: 7 bytes
           
           05 DECIMAL-EXPLICIT     PIC 9(5).99.
               *> Allows: 0 to 99,999.99
               *> Example: 12345.67
               *> Storage: 8 bytes (includes decimal point)
           
           05 SIGNED-NUMBER        PIC S9(5).
               *> Allows: -99,999 to +99,999
               *> Example: -12345
               *> Storage: 3 bytes (packed)
           
           05 BIG-NUMBER           PIC 9(18).
               *> For large calculations
               *> Example: 999,999,999,999,999,999
               *> Storage: 10 bytes
       
       *> ALPHANUMERIC DATA TYPES
       01 TEXT-DATA.
           05 SIMPLE-TEXT          PIC X(30).
               *> 30 characters of any type
               *> Example: "John Smith"
               *> Storage: 30 bytes
           
           05 VARIABLE-LENGTH      PIC X(100).
               *> Can store up to 100 characters
               *> Storage: 100 bytes (always allocated)
               *> Better to use VARYING in newer COBOL
           
           05 CODE-VALUE           PIC X(2).
               *> Example: "US", "NY", "AC"
               *> Storage: 2 bytes
       
       *> SPECIAL FORMATS
       01 FORMATTED-DATA.
           05 ZERO-SUPPRESSED      PIC Z(5)9.
               *> Suppresses leading zeros
               *> Value: 00123 displays as "   123"
           
           05 CURRENCY-FORMAT      PIC $9(5).99.
               *> Formats with dollar sign
               *> Value: 1234.56 displays as "$1234.56"
           
           05 ZERO-PADDED          PIC 09(5).
               *> Always shows leading zeros
               *> Value: 123 displays as "00123"
       
       *> COMPUTATIONAL TYPES (Optimized Storage)
       01 COMPUTATIONAL-DATA.
           05 COMP-BINARY          PIC 9(4) COMP.
               *> Binary format, faster calculations
               *> Storage: 2 bytes (vs 4 for normal)
           
           05 COMP-PACKED          PIC 9(5) COMP-3.
               *> Packed decimal format
               *> Storage: 3 bytes (vs 5 for normal)
           
           05 COMP-NATIVE          PIC 9(9) COMP-5.
               *> Native binary format
               *> Storage: 4 bytes (vs 9 for normal)
       
       *> DATE/TIME DATA
       01 DATE-TIME-DATA.
           05 DATE-CCYYMMDD        PIC 9(8).
               *> Example: 20240115 (2024-01-15)
           
           05 DATE-YYMMDD          PIC 9(6).
               *> Example: 240115
           
           05 TIME-HHMMSS          PIC 9(6).
               *> Example: 143025 (14:30:25)
       
       *> SPECIAL VARIABLES
       01 WS-SPECIAL-VARS.
           05 EOF-FLAG             PIC X VALUE 'N'.
               *> Named constant / flag
           
           05 RECORD-COUNT         PIC 9(6) VALUE 0.
               *> Initialized to 0
           
           05 TOTAL-AMOUNT         PIC 9(9)V99 VALUE 0.00.
               *> Decimal with initial value
```

**Storage Efficiency:**
```
Normal Storage vs COMP:
─────────────────────────────────────────────────
PIC 9(4)         → 4 bytes        (decimal)
PIC 9(4) COMP    → 2 bytes        (binary)
PIC 9(5) COMP-3  → 3 bytes        (packed)
PIC 9(9) COMP-5  → 4 bytes        (native binary)

For large files with millions of records, COMP types save 50%+ space!
```

### Practical Example: Data Type Usage

```cobol
       01 EMPLOYEE-RECORD.
           05 EMP-ID               PIC 9(7).
               *> Employee ID: 0 to 9,999,999
           
           05 EMP-NAME             PIC X(40).
               *> Name: up to 40 characters
           
           05 HOURLY-RATE          PIC 9(4)V99.
               *> $0.00 to $9,999.99
