## Control Structures

### Theory: Program Flow Control

**Types of Control:**
```
1. SEQUENTIAL
   Execute statements one after another

2. CONDITIONAL
   Execute based on condition (IF/EVALUATE)

3. ITERATIVE
   Repeat block while/until condition (PERFORM)

4. UNCONDITIONAL JUMP
   Jump to label (GO TO) - Generally avoided
```

### Practical Examples: All Control Structures

```cobol
       CONDITIONAL-STRUCTURES.
       
       SIMPLE-IF.
           IF AGE >= 18
               DISPLAY "Adult"
           END-IF.
       
       IF-ELSE.
           IF AGE >= 18
               DISPLAY "Adult"
           ELSE
               DISPLAY "Minor"
           END-IF.
       
       IF-ELSE-IF.
           IF AGE < 13
               DISPLAY "Child"
           ELSE IF AGE < 18
               DISPLAY "Teenager"
           ELSE IF AGE < 65
               DISPLAY "Adult"
           ELSE
               DISPLAY "Senior"
           END-IF
           END-IF
           END-IF.
       
       NESTED-IF.
           IF APPLICANT-VALID = 'Y'
               IF AGE >= 18
                   IF CREDIT-SCORE >= 700
                       DISPLAY "Approved"
                   ELSE
                       DISPLAY "Denied (Bad credit)"
                   END-IF
               ELSE
                   DISPLAY "Denied (Too young)"
               END-IF
           ELSE
               DISPLAY "Denied (Invalid application)"
           END-IF.
       
       EVALUATE-STATEMENT.
           *> Similar to switch/case in other languages
           EVALUATE GRADE-LETTER
               WHEN 'A'
                   MOVE 90 TO GRADE-VALUE
               WHEN 'B'
                   MOVE 80 TO GRADE-VALUE
               WHEN 'C'
                   MOVE 70 TO GRADE-VALUE
               WHEN 'D'
                   MOVE 60 TO GRADE-VALUE
               WHEN 'F'
                   MOVE 0 TO GRADE-VALUE
               WHEN OTHER
                   MOVE -1 TO GRADE-VALUE
           END-EVALUATE.
       
       EVALUATE-WITH-RANGES.
           EVALUATE TRUE
               WHEN SCORE >= 90
                   MOVE "A" TO GRADE-LETTER
               WHEN SCORE >= 80
                   MOVE "B" TO GRADE-LETTER
               WHEN SCORE >= 70
                   MOVE "C" TO GRADE-LETTER
               WHEN SCORE >= 60
                   MOVE "D" TO GRADE-LETTER
               WHEN OTHER
                   MOVE "F" TO GRADE-LETTER
           END-EVALUATE.
       
       ITERATIVE-STRUCTURES.
       
       PERFORM-N-TIMES.
           PERFORM 5 TIMES
               DISPLAY "Count: " COUNTER
               ADD 1 TO COUNTER
           END-PERFORM.
       
       PERFORM-UNTIL-LOOP.
           MOVE 1 TO COUNTER.
           PERFORM UNTIL COUNTER > 10
               DISPLAY "Count: " COUNTER
               ADD 1 TO COUNTER
           END-PERFORM.
       
       PERFORM-VARYING-LOOP.
           PERFORM VARYING INDEX FROM 1 BY 1
               UNTIL INDEX > 10
               DISPLAY "Index: " INDEX
           END-PERFORM.
           
           *> Double loop
           PERFORM VARYING ROW FROM 1 BY 1
               UNTIL ROW > 5
               PERFORM VARYING COL FROM 1 BY 1
                   UNTIL COL > 10
                   PERFORM PROCESS-CELL
               END-PERFORM
           END-PERFORM.
       
       PERFORM-VARYING-WITH-STEP.
           PERFORM VARYING NUM FROM 0 BY 2
               UNTIL NUM >= 20
               DISPLAY "Even: " NUM
           END-PERFORM.
```

### Theory: PERFORM vs GO TO

```
PERFORM (Modern, Structured):
├─ Creates structured blocks
├─ Returns control automatically
├─ Readable and maintainable
├─ Nesting is clean
└─ Preferred in modern COBOL

GO TO (Legacy, Avoided):
├─ Unconditional jump
├─ Creates "spaghetti code"
├─ Hard to follow logic
├─ Difficult to debug
└─ Only use for legacy maintenance

BEST PRACTICE: Never use GO TO in new code
```

