### Q4: Explain PERFORM vs GO TO. Why should you avoid GO TO?

**Answer:**

```
PERFORM:
├─ Structured, modern approach
├─ Clear program flow
├─ Automatic return from paragraph
├─ Nesting is organized
├─ Easy to debug
├─ Follows structured programming principles
└─ Industry standard (1980s onward)

GO TO:
├─ Legacy, unstructured approach
├─ Creates "spaghetti code"
├─ Jumps to arbitrary labels
├─ Hard to follow execution path
├─ Difficult to debug
├─ Violates structured programming
└─ Considered harmful (avoid always)
```

**Code Comparison:**

```cobol
       *> GOOD: Using PERFORM
       MAIN-PROCEDURE.
           PERFORM INITIALIZE-SYSTEM.
           PERFORM PROCESS-DATA.
           PERFORM CLEANUP.
           STOP RUN.
       
       INITIALIZE-SYSTEM.
           MOVE 0 TO COUNTER.
           OPEN INPUT DATA-FILE.
           *> Return automatically to MAIN-PROCEDURE
       
       PROCESS-DATA.
           PERFORM UNTIL EOF
               PERFORM PROCESS-RECORD
           END-PERFORM.
       
       *> BAD: Using GO TO (Don't do this!)
       BAD-PROCEDURE.
           MOVE 0 TO COUNTER.
           GO TO LABEL-1.
           
       LABEL-1.
           READ DATA-FILE.
           IF NOT EOF
               GO TO LABEL-2
           ELSE
               GO TO LABEL-3
           END-IF.
       
       LABEL-2.
           PERFORM PROCESS-RECORD.
           GO TO LABEL-1.
       
       LABEL-3.
           STOP RUN.
       *> Execution path is unclear and hard to follow!
```

**Why GO TO is Harmful:**

```
1. SPAGHETTI CODE
   └─ Hard to follow program execution

2. DIFFICULT TO DEBUG
   └─ Jumps all over the code

3. HARD TO REFACTOR
   └─ Changes break unexpected paths

4. UNMAINTAINABLE
   └─ Future developers struggle

5. VIOLATES BEST PRACTICES
   └─ Not industry standard

6. HARDER TO TEST
   └─ Difficult to isolate code paths
```

