## Real-World Challenges

### Q16: How would you handle Y2K or date-related issues in COBOL?

**Answer:**

```
Y2K PROBLEM:
├─ Dates stored as YY (2-digit year)
├─ Year 2000 interpreted as 1900
├─ Calculations fail
└─ Estimated cost to fix: $300 billion

MODERN DATE HANDLING:
├─ Use 4-digit years (YYYY)
├─ Use PICTURE 9(8) for YYYYMMDD
├─ Validate date ranges
└─ Test century boundaries
```

**Code Solutions:**

```cobol
       *> OLD WAY (Y2K Vulnerable)
       01 OLD-DATE-FORMAT.
           05 YY                PIC 9(2).
           05 MM                PIC 9(2).
           05 DD                PIC 9(2).
           *> Problem: Year 25 means 1925 or 2025?
       
       *> NEW WAY (Y2K Safe)
       01 NEW-DATE-FORMAT.
           05 CCYY              PIC 9(4).    *> 2024
           05 MM                PIC 9(2).    *> 01-12
           05 DD                PIC 9(2).    *> 01-31
       
       PROCEDURE DIVISION.
       
       CONVERT-OLD-TO-NEW.
           *> Convert 2-digit year to 4-digit
           IF YY < 30
               COMPUTE CCYY = 2000 + YY
           ELSE
               COMPUTE CCYY = 1900 + YY
           END-IF.
           
           *> Validation
           PERFORM VALIDATE-DATE.
       
       VALIDATE-DATE.
           IF MM < 1 OR MM > 12
               DISPLAY "Invalid month: " MM
               MOVE 'N' TO DATE-VALID
               GOBACK
           END-IF.
           
           EVALUATE MM
               WHEN 1
               WHEN 3
               WHEN 5
               WHEN 7
               WHEN 8
               WHEN 10
               WHEN 12
                   IF DD > 31
                       MOVE 'N' TO DATE-VALID
                   END-IF
               WHEN 4
               WHEN 6
               WHEN 9
               WHEN 11
                   IF DD > 30
                       MOVE 'N' TO DATE-VALID
                   END-IF
               WHEN 2
                   IF CCYY IS DIVISIBLE BY 4
                       IF DD > 29
                           MOVE 'N' TO DATE-VALID
                       END-IF
                   ELSE
                       IF DD > 28
                           MOVE 'N' TO DATE-VALID
                       END-IF
                   END-IF
           END-EVALUATE.
       
       DATE-ARITHMETIC.
           *> Add days to date
           MOVE FUNCTION DATE-OF-INTEGER
               (FUNCTION INTEGER-OF-DATE(CURRENT-DATE) + 30)
               TO NEW-DATE.
           
           *> Calculate days between dates
           MOVE FUNCTION INTEGER-OF-DATE(END-DATE) -
               FUNCTION INTEGER-OF-DATE(START-DATE)
               TO DAYS-DIFFERENCE.
       
